{"timestamp":"2026-02-02T07:03:31.417Z","tool":"Write","command":"{\"file_path\":\"C:/Users/rasche_j/Documents/workspace/compost-coordinator/GOVERNANCE.md\",\"content\":\"# Governance & Legal Structure\\n\\n## Mission\\n\\n**Build soil, build skills, build community.**\\n\\nTurn neighborhood waste into garden gold while teaching youth regenerative land stewardship.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Structure: Worker Co-op\\n\\nA worker-owned cooperative where profits go back to members (workers), not external shareholders.\\n\\n### Why Co-op (not Nonprofit)\\n\\n| Question | Co-op | Nonprofit 501(c)(3) |\\n|----------|-------|---------------------|\\n| Can members get paid well? | Yes - that's the point | Limited, must be \\\"reasonable\\\" |\\n| Where do profits go? | Back to members | Must stay in organization |\\n| Who controls it? | Members vote | Board (may not include workers) |\\n| Tax-deductible donations? | No | Yes |\\n| Complexity to form? | Moderate | High |\\n\\nWe want youth earning $50+/hr. That's a co-op.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Governance Phases\\n\\n### Phase 1: Founder-Controlled (Now)\\n\\n**Operator:** You (the adult setting this up)\\n\\n- All decision authority rests with founding operator\\n- You invest capital, build systems, take risk\\n- Youth workers are paid members but don't govern yet\\n- Simple, fast, appropriate for startup phase\\n\\n**Transition trigger:** When 3+ youth workers are active for 6+ months\\n\\n### Phase 2: Advisory Board\\n\\n- Founding operator + 2-3 youth representatives\\n- Youth advise on operations, scheduling, pricing\\n- Operator retains final decision authority\\n- Prepares youth for governance responsibility\\n\\n**Transition trigger:** When revenue exceeds $10k/year or 5+ active workers\\n\\n### Phase 3: Democratic Co-op\\n\\n- 1 member = 1 vote on major decisions\\n- Elected board handles day-to-day\\n- Members vote on: pricing, new services, equipment over $500, bylaws changes\\n- Founding operator becomes one vote among many (or advisory role)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Roles\\n\\n### Founding Operator (You)\\n\\n- Infrastructure setup and maintenance\\n- Training and supervision\\n- Accounting and compliance\\n- Customer acquisition systems\\n- Equipment purchasing\\n- Rate: $50/hr consulting (paid by co-op when revenue allows)\\n\\n### Youth Workers\\n\\n- Collection routes\\n- Composting operations\\n- Customer service\\n- Sales (with training)\\n- Rate: $50+/hr effective (varies by task efficiency)\\n\\n### Customers/Members\\n\\n- Provide materials (food waste, cardboard, yard waste)\\n- Pay subscription ($25/mo or $250/yr)\\n- Receive compost giveback (10 gal/year)\\n- No governance rights (they're customers, not worker-members)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Legal Path\\n\\n### Now: Informal / DBA\\n\\n- File DBA (\\\"Doing Business As\\\") in Michigan: ~$20\\n- Operate under your name with a business alias\\n- Simple, start immediately\\n- You're personally liable (mitigate with waivers + homeowner's insurance check)\\n\\n### Later: Formal Co-op\\n\\nWhen revenue justifies paperwork:\\n\\n1. **Michigan Cooperative Corporations Act** - file Articles of Incorporation\\n2. **Draft bylaws** - governance, membership, profit distribution\\n3. **EIN** - get federal tax ID\\n4. **Bank account** - in co-op's name\\n5. **Insurance** - general liability policy\\n\\nEstimated cost: $200-500 in filing fees, or ~$1000 with lawyer review\\n\\n### Alternative: Fiscal Sponsorship\\n\\nPartner with existing 501(c)(3) nonprofit:\\n- They provide legal/tax umbrella\\n- Their insurance covers your activities\\n- You keep 90-95% of funds\\n- Good for receiving grants or tax-deductible donations\\n- 5-10% admin fee\\n\\nPotential sponsors:\\n- Local community foundations\\n- Environmental nonprofits\\n- MSU Extension / 4-H programs\\n- Land trusts\\n- Churches with community gardens\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Insurance & Liability\\n\\n### Risks\\n\\n| Risk | Scenario | Protection |\\n|------|----------|------------|\\n| Worker injury on your property | Kid hurts themselves with pitchfork | Homeowner's insurance (check policy) + parent waiver |\\n| Worker injury on customer property | ATV accident during delivery | General liability policy (~$400/yr) |\\n| Property damage | Mower hits fence | General liability |\\n| Product liability | \\\"Your compost killed my tomatoes\\\" | General liability (rare claims) |\\n\\n### Minimum Viable Protection\\n\\n1. **Check homeowner's policy** - many cover \\\"incidental business\\\" up to $X\\n2. **Parent waivers** - for all youth workers\\n3. **Customer service agreements** - liability acknowledgment\\n4. **General liability** - when doing lawn work on others' property (~$30-50/mo)\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Decision Framework\\n\\n### Operator Decides (Phase 1-2)\\n\\n- Equipment purchases\\n- Pricing\\n- Route design\\n- Customer acceptance\\n- Worker training requirements\\n- Safety rules\\n\\n### Members Vote (Phase 3)\\n\\n- Pricing changes\\n- New service offerings\\n- Equipment purchases over $500\\n- Bylaw amendments\\n- Admission of new members\\n- Distribution of surplus profits\\n\\n### Always Require Consensus\\n\\n- Safety policy changes\\n- Removal of a member\\n- Dissolution of co-op\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Profit Distribution\\n\\nAfter paying:\\n1. Operating expenses (fuel, supplies, insurance)\\n2. Equipment maintenance/replacement fund (10% of revenue)\\n3. Worker payments (hourly rate × hours worked)\\n\\nRemaining surplus distributed:\\n- 50% to workers (proportional to hours worked)\\n- 30% to equipment/growth fund\\n- 20% to founding operator (infrastructure maintenance)\\n\\nPercentages adjustable by member vote in Phase 3.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Exit / Dissolution\\n\\nIf co-op dissolves:\\n1. Pay all outstanding debts\\n2. Return customer deposits (if any)\\n3. Sell equipment, distribute proceeds to members by hours worked\\n4. Remaining assets donated to local environmental nonprofit\\n\\nNo member can \\\"sell their share\\\" - this is a worker co-op, not an investment.\\n\"}","result":"unknown","duration_ms":0}
