{"timestamp":"2026-02-02T15:35:08.410Z","tool":"Task","command":"{\"description\":\"Research flow viz architecture\",\"prompt\":\"Research and compare modern approaches for building an interactive flow diagram with these requirements:\\n\\n**Core Requirements:**\\n1. Animated icons moving along edges (multiple icons per edge, bidirectional)\\n2. Clickable edges and nodes with detail panels\\n3. Drag-and-drop repositioning\\n4. Smooth performance (60fps animations)\\n5. Edge width encoding frequency\\n6. SVG path-based rendering\\n7. Responsive positioning\\n\\n**Evaluate these options:**\\n\\n**Option 1: React Flow (xyflow)**\\n- Pros/cons for this use case\\n- Icon animation support\\n- Custom edge rendering capabilities\\n- Performance characteristics\\n- Learning curve\\n- Bundle size\\n\\n**Option 2: D3.js**\\n- Pros/cons for custom flow visualizations\\n- Path animation patterns\\n- Force-directed vs manual positioning\\n- Complexity for this use case\\n- Performance\\n\\n**Option 3: Vanilla JS + SVG (current approach)**\\n- What it would take to add icon animation\\n- Performance limitations\\n- Maintainability concerns\\n- When this is the right choice\\n\\n**Option 4: Canvas-based (Konva, Fabric.js)**\\n- Performance for animations\\n- Interaction complexity\\n- SVG vs Canvas trade-offs\\n\\n**Option 5: Specialized libraries (Cytoscape, GoJS, yFiles)**\\n- Licensing/cost\\n- Overkill for this use case?\\n\\nFocus on:\\n- Which approach best handles **animated icons on edges**\\n- Which has cleanest **edge interaction model**\\n- Which provides **best developer experience** for iteration\\n- Performance at scale (if we add 20+ nodes/50+ edges later)\\n\\nUse web search to find:\\n- Benchmarks comparing these approaches\\n- Example implementations of animated edge icons\\n- Community recommendations for 2026\\n- Real-world projects using each approach\",\"subagent_type\":\"Explore\"}","result":"unknown","duration_ms":0}
